David Damerell wrote:
> Quoting  Doug Freyburger  <dfreybur@yahoo.com>:
>
>>slipping on ice?  Switching from capital letter rushed motion to
>>individual square motion sure changed slipping on ice.
>
> I cannot see that it makes any difference whatsoever.

Run goes in a direction until something changes that direction and
continues until progress is blocked.  Try running in a direction in the
mines to see how that works, especially on levels where digging has
happened.  The run will follow curves in the wall and sometimes end up
in a surprising location very far from a straight line.

Near as I can tell a slip is treated like a direction change.  A run
with a slip can end up very far from a straight line and on a water
level that's very often in a pool.

In comparison if I type each square by hand I'll get a slip message but
my very next move is to specify the exact direction to go next.

Slips happen within a square but they appear to change the direction
during a run.  Since walking a square at a time sets the direction every
time direction does not matter in that sense.

> It is probably just
> because using a run command almost inevitably leads to a slip at some
> point.

Exactly.  And what happens then is very different running or not.

> It is also possible that you have become more disciplined about
> obtaining cold resistance, which reduces the rate of slips - or that you
> happened to be wearing snow boots recently.

Both could account for how often a slip occurs but not what happens
after a slip.  If there are no slips then a run on the ice continues to
the desired location and the event is easily forgotten.  If there is a
slip on the ice the character ends up in a very different location in a
pool and the event is easily remembered.  I got that bit.


